/*
  core/stressors/habituation.spthy -- f_U for sigma8 Habituation, CEREMONY-AGNOSTIC.

  Migrated to the agnostic interface (AGNOSTIC_STRESSOR_INTERFACE.md). Like sigma9 this is a DENSITY
  detector (repeat-exposure count), not a per-step workload lookup, so it reads no lexicon. The old
  rule counted handled-attentive approvals -- two distinct !Approved(P,_,'Attentive'). This version
  counts the OBJECTIVE approval steps the ceremony presents -- two distinct !Step(P,_,'confirm') -- so
  it reads the SAME Layer-1 vocabulary as every other migrated detector and names no ceremony fact.
  'confirm' is a generic interaction-taxonomy category (accept/dismiss a prompt), distinct from sigma9's
  'decide' (choose among options), so the two density detectors never cross-trigger.

  Warning / security habituation (Anderson & Vance et al., CHI 2015; MFA prompt-bombing): after >= k
  approval prompts the user reflexively approves -> SetMask Habituated (habituated_approve auto-approves
  ANY prompt, incl. an injected one -- what prompt-bombing weaponizes). THRESHOLD k = 2 is the HCI
  parameter, realised as two !Step premises + Neq distinctness (bump k by adding/removing a premise).
  Bounded once per party (OnceHabituate) so the mask-history interval reasoning terminates.

  NB: counting is at PRESENT time (prompt shown), not handle time. The old !Approved count additionally
  required the priors were handled 'Attentive'; dropping that is faithful to prompt-bombing (you
  habituate from being BOMBED with prompts) and preserves the lemmas -- the shutout block
  (ShutoutBlocksAutoApprove) and recovery causality (mask_state gates) are independent of how sigma8
  triggers. Inequality must NOT share a theory with sigma9's identically-named restriction (kept apart:
  sigma8 in P2/P6, sigma9 in P4/S1).
*/

rule Trigger_Habituation:
    [ !Step(P, pid1, 'confirm'), !Step(P, pid2, 'confirm'), !StressEnable(P) ]
  --[ Habituate(P), Neq(pid1, pid2), SetMask(P,'Habituated') ]->
    [ ]

restriction Inequality:
  "All x #i. Neq(x,x)@i ==> F"

restriction OnceHabituate:
  "All p #i #j. Habituate(p)@i & Habituate(p)@j ==> #i = #j"
